# rediacc_ci allowlist golden
# reader: bash:.ci/scripts/lib/blocker-validator.sh::parse_blockered_list
# command: parse_blockered_list <corpus> A B '#'; for k in ${!B[@]}; printf '%s\t%s\n'; LC_ALL=C sort
# projection: pairs
# source: .unverified-download-allowlist
# corpus: corpus/unverified-download-allowlist.list
# comment-char: #
# corpus-sha256: 0137ac19a315d1f7cdb0bb5d02e6a066c527d649e8baaa3904ce96265b62b145
# ---
awscli.amazonaws.com	AWS publishes no sha256 for the CLI bundle -- the .sha256 URL returns 404 -- only a GPG .sig, which needs their signing key imported first; revisit if a checksum ever appears
claude.ai/install.sh	piped straight into bash, same shape as the nvm installer above; the vendor publishes no pinned, checksummed release artifact to use instead
download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg	this is an apt KEYRING, and apt itself verifies every package installed from that repo against it via signed-by, so the packages are covered even though this fetch is not
raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm	piped straight into bash, so there is no artifact on disk to hash; only vendoring the installer script into this repo would make it verifiable
